(7215063, 1110967, 96751, '<td valign="top">Congrats, Judi!!!  What an honor to share a ribbon with you! How funky is that!?  I don''t know if I''ve seen a ribbon tie before :)<br/> <br/>I had a pretty good idea this was yours, as I think "mystery" is a good word to describe your work.  And I also just noticed there seems to be a figure in the middle of the glow! More mystery</td>', 'Congrats, Judi!!!  What an honor to share a ribbon with you! How funky is that!?  I don''t know if I''ve seen a ribbon tie before :) I had a pretty good idea this was yours, as I think "mystery" is a good word to describe your work.  And I also just noticed there seems to be a figure in the middle of the glow! More mystery', '2014-05-09 11:04:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(7215075, 1111532, 96751, '<td valign="top">You sure do know how to keep your private life PRIVATE! Congratulations on the impending addition to the family. How incredibly exciting!!!!<br/> <br/>Oh, and of course, congrats on the blue :)<br/> <br/>ETA - At the aquarium, I am also fascinated by the jellies. Surreal and ethereal.  <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2014-05-09 10:11:17.</i></td>', 'You sure do know how to keep your private life PRIVATE! Congratulations on the impending addition to the family. How incredibly exciting!!!! Oh, and of course, congrats on the blue :) ETA - At the aquarium, I am also fascinated by the jellies. Surreal and ethereal.  ', '2014-05-09 11:10:07.000000', '2014-05-09 11:11:17.000000', 0),
(7215079, 1110586, 30364, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by skewsme:</b></div><hr/><i> how do their antlers decide to curve like that?  really like the composition with the two in the foreground and the other longhorn-looking animals in the distance. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Not antlers but horns; these are mature male impala antelopes.  The horns take on this lovely compound curve, rather like a lyre when viewed from front-on, beginning in their second year.  The ''longhorn-looking animals'' are wildebeest, also called gnu though rarely locally.  They''re antelopes too.</td>', 'Originally posted by skewsme: how do their antlers decide to curve like that?  really like the composition with the two in the foreground and the other longhorn-looking animals in the distance.  Not antlers but horns; these are mature male impala antelopes.  The horns take on this lovely compound curve, rather like a lyre when viewed from front-on, beginning in their second year.  The ''longhorn-looking animals'' are wildebeest, also called gnu though rarely locally.  They''re antelopes too.', '2014-05-09 11:16:53.000000', NULL, 0),
